{
  // Output filename and BoardLoader data-variable name:
  //   boardName "RP2040" -> designer/boards/RP2040/RP2040.json -> const RP2040Data = {...}
  "boardName": "RP2040",

  // Fallback human-readable name shown in the designer UI.  Overridden
  // by the matching `<id>.name=` entry in a sibling-or-ancestor
  // boards.txt when one is present (matched via `<id>.build.variant=`
  // against this variant's directory name) — for this board that's
  // "iLabs Challenger NB 2040 WiFi" (variants/rp2040/boards.txt).
  "displayName": "Raspberry Pi Pico (RP2040)",

  // Same parser dialect as every other RP2040 board — see
  // ../board.json for the full rationale.
  "family": "rp2040",

  "inputOnlyGpios": [],

  // Default transports — Serial only. This board's own pins_arduino.h
  // (top-of-file pfodWeb NOTE, already present before this audit) documents
  // why: it carries an add-on ESP8285 WiFi co-processor reached over a
  // plain UART (Serial2 / GPIO4-5) plus two GPIO control lines
  // (PIN_ESP8285_MODE/PIN_ESP8285_RST), NOT the native RP2040 + CYW43439
  // combo that Pico W / rp2040w-bucket boards use (matches the sibling
  // challenger_2040_wifi board exactly — same ESP8285 co-processor wiring,
  // same PIN_SERIAL2_TX/RX=4/5, PIN_ESP8285_MODE=13, PIN_ESP8285_RST=19,
  // and the same build.extra_flags=-DWIFIESPAT2 in boards.txt). This board
  // would need its own dedicated AT-command/co-processor .ino templates
  // before it could genuinely offer tcp/http/ble connections — also listed
  // by name alongside challenger_2040_wifi in the rp2040w chip bucket's own
  // board.json comment as one of the boards deliberately excluded from that
  // bucket for this reason.
  "connections": ["serial"],

  "pinNotes": {},

  // pfodWeb NOTE (dedicated-hardware pin-exclusion + LED/NeoPixel tagging
  // audit, 2026-07-13):
  //
  // Board: iLabs "Challenger NB RP2040 WiFi" (Adafruit Feather form
  // factor). "NB" = "No Battery" — confirmed via the vendor datasheet: this
  // is a variant of the regular Challenger RP2040 WiFi board with the
  // battery connector and Li-Po charging circuit removed, and "extra GPIO
  // pins added" in that freed-up board area instead. It is NOT an NB-IoT
  // cellular variant (no cellular modem of any kind is present) — verified
  // directly against the datasheet text and confirmed by boards.txt
  // (build.extra_flags=-DWIFIESPAT2, the same ESP8285 AT-firmware flag used
  // by the plain challenger_2040_wifi board; no modem-related library flag
  // anywhere in this board's boards.txt entry).
  //
  // Sources consulted:
  //   1. https://ilabs.se/challenger-nb-rp2040-wifi-datasheet/ (official
  //      vendor datasheet, fetched directly) — confirms: RP2040 + 8MB
  //      flash; WiFi section "based on the Espressif ESP8285 chip...
  //      connected to the microcontroller using a UART channel" driven by
  //      AT-commands; onboard user LED "connected to pin D15 (LED_BUILTIN)
  //      and can easily be controlled by the user program" (LED_BUILTIN
  //      resolves unambiguously via this board's own pins_arduino.h
  //      `#define LED_BUILTIN PIN_LED` / `#define PIN_LED (12u)` to GPIO12,
  //      independent of any iLabs silkscreen "Dn" numbering ambiguity);
  //      confirms the "No Battery" design removes the battery
  //      connector/charge circuit and adds "extra GPIO pins" in its place;
  //      no onboard pushbutton mentioned; does not explicitly state the
  //      NeoPixel's GPIO number.
  //   2. CircuitPython's board "challenger_nb_rp2040_wifi" pins.c
  //      (github.com/adafruit/circuitpython, ports/raspberrypi/boards/
  //      challenger_nb_rp2040_wifi/pins.c, fetched directly) —
  //      independently confirms `board.LED` -> GPIO12, `board.NEOPIXEL` ->
  //      GPIO11, `board.WIFI_MODE` -> GPIO13, `board.WIFI_RESET` -> GPIO19,
  //      `board.A5` -> GPIO21 (matching this board's own PIN_SPI0_SS=21,
  //      a normal dual-use analog/SPI-chip-select breakout pin, not a
  //      dedicated-hardware collision). No `board.BUTTON` defined anywhere
  //      in the file — corroborating the datasheet's silence on a
  //      pushbutton.
  //   3. This board's own pins_arduino.h (variants/rp2040/rp2040/
  //      challenger_nb_2040_wifi/pins_arduino.h) — already carries a
  //      pre-existing top-of-file pfodWeb NOTE re: the ESP8285 co-processor
  //      and Serial-only bucketing. Verified directly: PIN_LED=(12u),
  //      NEOPIXEL=(11u), PIN_SERIAL2_TX=(4u)/PIN_SERIAL2_RX=(5u),
  //      PIN_ESP8285_MODE=(13u), PIN_ESP8285_RST=(19u) — all four ESP8285
  //      control/UART GPIOs (4, 5, 13, 19) are confirmed absent from the
  //      D0-D17/A0-A5 alias list (D-aliases resolve to GPIOs {16,17,24,23,
  //      22,2,3,0,1,6,7,8,9,10,14,15,18,20}; A-aliases to {26,27,28,29,25,
  //      21}) — matching the sibling challenger_2040_wifi board's own
  //      equivalent exclusion.
  //   4. Sibling precedent: variants/rp2040/rp2040/challenger_2040_wifi/
  //      board.json (already audited this session) — identical ESP8285
  //      wiring (GPIO4/5/13/19), identical PIN_LED=12/NEOPIXEL=11, and its
  //      own datasheet (https://ilabs.se/challenger-rp2040-wifi-datasheet/)
  //      explicitly states "Active level: High" for the shared onboard LED
  //      reference-circuit design reused across this entire iLabs
  //      Challenger RP2040 product line — this board's own datasheet does
  //      not restate the polarity, so led_high here is a cross-referenced
  //      inference from the identical shared circuit on the direct sibling
  //      board, consistent with every other RP2040 board audited in this
  //      family to date (all confirmed or inferred active-high, none
  //      active-low).
  //
  // Full findings/sources trail:
  // boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2040/challenger_nb_2040_wifi/notes.txt
  //
  // Per-pin reasoning:
  //   GPIO4  - PIN_SERIAL2_TX / PIN_ESP_TX - onboard ESP8285 WiFi
  //            co-processor's dedicated UART1 TX line, not general
  //            purpose. No SPI/I2C bus alias on this pin, so no
  //            build_boards.js auto-append to strip — capabilities left
  //            empty outright.
  //   GPIO5  - PIN_SERIAL2_RX / PIN_ESP_RX - ESP8285 co-processor UART1
  //            RX. Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO13 - PIN_ESP8285_MODE / PIN_ESP_MODE - ESP8285 flash-mode
  //            select line, driven by the RP2040 during ESP8285 firmware
  //            updates. Not general-purpose. Independently confirmed as
  //            board.WIFI_MODE in CircuitPython.
  //   GPIO19 - PIN_ESP8285_RST / PIN_ESP_RST - ESP8285 active-low reset
  //            line, driven by the RP2040. Not general-purpose.
  //            Independently confirmed as board.WIFI_RESET in
  //            CircuitPython.
  //   GPIO11 - NEOPIXEL - onboard WS2812 addressable RGB status pixel.
  //            Tagged "led_neopixel" (not led_high/led_low, since it is
  //            one addressable pixel, not a simple on/off LED) — matches
  //            this repo's established led_neopixel precedent (e.g.
  //            challenger_2040_wifi/board.json GPIO11, sea_picro/
  //            board.json GPIO25). pwm_output kept per the same
  //            precedent (arduino-pico's analogWrite() works on any
  //            RP2040 GPIO); digital_input dropped.
  //   GPIO12 - PIN_LED / LED_BUILTIN - onboard user LED, confirmed by the
  //            vendor datasheet's own "D15 (LED_BUILTIN)" text (LED_BUILTIN
  //            resolves via this file's own #define to GPIO12) and
  //            independently by CircuitPython's board.LED=GPIO12. Tagged
  //            led_high per the sibling challenger_2040_wifi board's own
  //            datasheet ("Active level: High" for the identical shared
  //            reference LED circuit); digital_input dropped, pwm_output
  //            kept for brightness dimming.
  //
  // No onboard user button beyond BOOTSEL (not a normal GPIO, out of scope
  // per the same reasoning already documented in rpipico/board.json) —
  // confirmed no separate pushbutton exists on this board via both the
  // vendor datasheet and CircuitPython's pins.c (no board.BUTTON defined).
  //
  // GPIO4/5/13/19 carry no SPI/I2C/other bus alias on this board, so the
  // build_boards.js unconditional bus-capability auto-append (the
  // "confirmed button/LED pin also aliased as SDA/SCL/SS/MOSI/MISO/SCK"
  // bug class flagged for this audit) does not apply here — no
  // pins_arduino.h edit was needed. GPIO11/12 (NeoPixel/LED) likewise
  // carry no SPI/I2C/other bus alias (Wire0 lives on GPIO0-1, SPI0 on
  // GPIO21-24).
  //
  // GPIO14, 15, 18, 20 (D14-D17) — the "extra GPIO pins" the datasheet
  // says were added in place of the removed battery connector/charge
  // circuit — carry no dedicated-hardware function of any kind (confirmed
  // via both the datasheet and CircuitPython's pins.c, which expose them
  // as plain board.D14-board.D17 general-purpose pins). Left with full
  // default capabilities via the empty chip-level chipGpios — no override
  // needed.
  "chipGpios": {
    "4": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SERIAL2_TX / PIN_ESP_TX - onboard ESP8285 WiFi co-processor's dedicated UART1 TX line, not general-purpose." },
    "5": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_SERIAL2_RX / PIN_ESP_RX - onboard ESP8285 WiFi co-processor's dedicated UART1 RX line, not general-purpose." },
    "13": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_ESP8285_MODE / PIN_ESP_MODE - onboard ESP8285 WiFi co-processor's flash-mode select line, driven by the RP2040 during firmware updates. Not general-purpose. Confirmed as CircuitPython's board.WIFI_MODE." },
    "19": { "capabilities": [], "note": "PIN_ESP8285_RST / PIN_ESP_RST - onboard ESP8285 WiFi co-processor's active-low reset line, driven by the RP2040. Not general-purpose. Confirmed as CircuitPython's board.WIFI_RESET." },
    "11": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "led_neopixel", "pwm_output"], "note": "NEOPIXEL - onboard WS2812 addressable RGB status LED. Confirmed via CircuitPython's board.NEOPIXEL=GP11 definition, matching this board's own NEOPIXEL=(11u)." },
    "12": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "pwm_output", "led_high"], "note": "PIN_LED / LED_BUILTIN - onboard user LED, confirmed by the vendor datasheet's own \"D15 (LED_BUILTIN)\" text and CircuitPython's board.LED=GP12 definition. Active-high polarity cross-referenced from the sibling challenger_2040_wifi board's datasheet (\"Active level: High\" for the identical shared reference LED circuit reused across this iLabs product line)." }
  },

  // Same 8-bit analogWrite() default as every other RP2040/RP2350 board.
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },

  // No hardware DAC on RP2040/RP2350.
  "dac": {},

  // RP2040's one 12-bit ADC (0-4095), ~3.3V reference — same as every
  // other RP2040 board. Matches this board's own pins_arduino.h
  // (NUM_ANALOG_INPUTS=4, ADC_RESOLUTION=12u).
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" }
}
